{
  "doc": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 1,
        "id": "release-signing-key-custody-and-recovery-runbook",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Release-signing key custody and recovery runbook"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The operational runbook for the release-signing identity - asset #2 in the security plan and the true code-push keystone of the fleet."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "After SEC-14/SEC-15 the "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "f5w_services"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " box can no longer push code to the ~30 customer sites, but whatever signs a release can: a single ed25519 signature turns any plugin zip into fleet-wide PHP execution, and via SEC-15's transitive anchoring the visitor script channel too. The signing identity therefore gets the custody, recovery, and succession discipline the box used to. This runbook is the procedure a second operator who has never run a release can follow; the design rationale lives in SEC-18 ("
          },
          {
            "kind": "link",
            "href": "../wiki/security-plan.md",
            "rel": "doc",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "knowledge/wiki/security-plan.md"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ") and decision P22 ("
          },
          {
            "kind": "link",
            "href": "../wiki/wp-umami-stats-plugin.md",
            "rel": "doc",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "knowledge/wiki/wp-umami-stats-plugin.md"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "), which this document operationalizes without diverging from."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "tone": "sec",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Standing rule, before anything below: no key material ever enters this repo, the box, or CI. This runbook references 1Password items by name, never their contents."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "the-asset-and-what-a-compromise-buys",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The asset and what a compromise buys"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "list",
        "items": [
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "The asset is the ed25519 "
              },
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "signing identity"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ", not a machine: the private key plus whatever host holds the unlocked 1Password agent at signing time. That host, for the moment it signs, IS the keystone."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "One valid signature = a release the whole fleet installs and executes - "
              },
              {
                "kind": "link",
                "href": "#what-a-compromise-buys",
                "rel": "detail",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "what a compromise buys"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " carries the blast radius and the accepted residual."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "key-custody",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Key custody"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "list",
        "items": [
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "Key #1 private"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " lives ONLY in 1Password, biometric-gated. It is never written to disk in plaintext, never copied to the box ("
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "f5w_services"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "), and never placed in CI. Signing happens on a clean operator host with the 1Password agent unlocked for that one operation."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "Two public keys are embedded from day one"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (SEC-14), checked by "
              },
              {
                "kind": "link",
                "href": "#how-or-verification-works",
                "rel": "detail",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "OR-verification"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". Two keys exist so rotation is ceremony-free (see Rotation)."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "Key #2 private stays OFFLINE"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (SR-7 - "
              },
              {
                "kind": "link",
                "href": "#why-key-2-stays-offline",
                "rel": "detail",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "why offline"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "). Key #2 is the break-glass / rotation reserve: its private half lives on an offline medium and comes online only to cut a recovery or rotation release."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "What is signed:"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " both the plugin "
              },
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "zip"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " and the "
              },
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "manifest JSON"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " carry a detached ed25519 signature, each published at "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "\u003cartifact-url\u003e.sig"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (SR-5 - "
              },
              {
                "kind": "link",
                "href": "#why-the-manifest-is-signed-too",
                "rel": "detail",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "why the manifest too"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "). Custody covers signing both artifacts in the same ceremony."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "where-the-keys-and-access-live-the-successors-map",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Where the keys and access live (the successor's map)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "A successor recovering the fleet needs all of the following. Keep this list current; it is the single index of what a release operator must be able to reach."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "list",
        "items": [
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "Key #1 private"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " - 1Password item "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "[VERIFY: item name]"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ", biometric-gated, in vault "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "[VERIFY: vault]"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". Grant a successor access to this vault to make succession real."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "Key #2 private"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " - offline at "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "[VERIFY: offline location/medium]"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (e.g. an encrypted offline drive in physical custody). Not in 1Password's online vault, not on the laptop."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "Embedded public-key set"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " - shipped inside the plugin. Current state (footfall-t3z / footfall-rd5): the set ships EMPTY as "
              },
              {
                "kind": "link",
                "href": "#the-empty-fail-closed-seed",
                "rel": "detail",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "a fail-closed seed"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " - every candidate update is refused until the real public keys are embedded. There is no live PRODUCTION signing key to lose yet; this runbook governs the moment the real keypair is minted onward. Minting the keypair and embedding the two real public keys is the ceremony that arms the channel."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "InfiniteWP admin"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " - the fleet-management console that can push a plugin to all sites manually. 1Password item "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "[VERIFY: IWP admin item]"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". This is the recovery channel (below)."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "The release pipeline"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " - footfall-8au built the mechanics ("
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "wp-plugin/tools/release-build-sign.php"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " + the CI cross-check in "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "release.yml"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ") that build the signed zip + manifest and emit the "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": ".sig"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " files; this runbook does not duplicate it. The signing step there is the custody-bound step described here. Minting the first keypair and embedding its public half are the operator's ceremony, completed when the channel goes live (footfall-t3z)."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "normal-signing-ceremony-context-not-the-pipeline",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Normal signing ceremony (context, not the pipeline)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Custody only holds if the routine ceremony respects it. The release is "
          },
          {
            "kind": "strong",
            "text": "built locally"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " in a pinned toolchain and the "
          },
          {
            "kind": "strong",
            "text": "local artifact is signed"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " (SEC-17, P21): a signature attests origin, not build integrity, so signing whatever CI produced would launder a compromised runner into a valid release. CI runs the same commit as an independent cross-check (per-file hashes of the unpacked tree must match), never as the source of signed bytes. The operator, on a clean host, unlocks key #1 in 1Password, signs the zip and the manifest, and publishes each "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "\u003cartifact-url\u003e.sig"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ". No hand-built zips; no unsigned zip ever serves."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Concretely, the operator runs "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "make plugin-build"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", then "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "make plugin-release"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " with the key injected for one operation - "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "F5W_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY=\"$(op read 'op://\u003cvault\u003e/\u003citem\u003e/\u003cfield\u003e')\" make plugin-release"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "list",
        "items": [
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "which writes the zip, manifest, and both detached signatures to "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "dist/"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "; the key never touches disk or CI and is zeroed after use. "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "plugin-release"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " derives "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "--previous-version"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " from the highest existing "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "v*"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " git tag (the "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "none"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " sentinel for the first release) and refuses a version that is not strictly greater than it"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "a build-time forgot-to-bump fail-fast (footfall-7ib) that is MANDATORY once the signing key is present, so a non-increased release fails here instead of silently never installing across the fleet (the runtime anti-rollback in the verifier is what stops an attacker replaying an old release; this build-time guard is an operational fail-fast, not a trust control, and the prior version comes from a local tag, never from the served manifest). Before serving, "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "release-build-sign.php crosscheck"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " diffs the local per-file hashes against CI's published "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": ".sha256"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ". The step-by-step is the README \"Releasing the plugin\" section; this runbook governs the custody around it."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "First real release: the embedded trust anchor ("
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "UpdateVerifier::$trustedPublicKeysBase64"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ") ships empty, so before the first signed release the operator mints the keypair (per \"Assemble the replacement key\" / rotation below - generate on a clean host, private half into 1Password, key #2's private half offline) and edits the anchor to the two real public halves. An empty anchor fails closed (a signed release would install nowhere), so this step is a natural prerequisite, not a separate gate."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "tone": "info",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Planned change (footfall-gpb, decided 2026-07-09; gated on 0.1-testing completion, not yet in effect): the build step ("
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "make plugin-build"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ") relocates to Carl (the build VM, Mac-orchestrated over SSH, like the image build); the Mac runs only the php-only "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "make plugin-release"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " sign step against the rsync'd tree, with signing HARD-GATED on "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "crosscheck"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " matching CI's published per-file hashes (a divergence then blocks signing, no longer merely alarms). The signing key and this ceremony stay on the Mac exactly as described - only where the unsigned bytes are built moves. Trust-model rationale: decision P24 / the SEC-17 refinement ("
              },
              {
                "kind": "link",
                "href": "../wiki/security-plan.md",
                "rel": "doc",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "knowledge/wiki/security-plan.md"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "). Until that pipeline ships, the local-build-and-sign flow above is current."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "key-loss--compromise-recovery-the-infinitewp-escape-hatch",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Key-loss / compromise recovery (the InfiniteWP escape hatch)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The recovery path does NOT depend on the lost key ("
          },
          {
            "kind": "link",
            "href": "#why-infinitewp-is-the-escape-hatch",
            "rel": "detail",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "why InfiniteWP is the escape hatch"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "), and the recovery release moves the fleet onto a trust root the attacker cannot feed ("
          },
          {
            "kind": "link",
            "href": "#re-anchoring-mechanics",
            "rel": "detail",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "re-anchoring mechanics"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ")."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Run this sequence in order. It is written to be unambiguous to a second operator."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "steps",
        "steps": [
          {
            "title": "Stop the bleeding.",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Do not sign anything else with the lost or compromised key. If compromise (not mere loss) is suspected, treat every release that key could have signed since the suspected compromise as suspect and plan to supersede it in step 5."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Assemble the replacement key.",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Retrieve key #2's private half from offline storage, OR mint a fresh ed25519 keypair on a clean host. The replacement's PUBLIC half is what the recovery release will embed."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Build a recovery release",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "locally whose embedded public-key set DROPS the lost/compromised key and INCLUDES the replacement public key. Build it the normal local way (SEC-17); only the embedded key set and the signing key differ."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Sign it",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "with the surviving / replacement private key (zip + manifest), on a clean host."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Push it to the whole fleet via InfiniteWP",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ": manual plugin upload + bulk install/activate across all ~30 sites. This bypasses the auto-update channel, which the lost key can no longer drive. (If specific releases were marked suspect in step 1, this is also the release that supersedes them.)"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Confirm re-anchoring.",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Verify in InfiniteWP's fleet version view that every site is on the recovery version. From this release on, the fleet trusts only the new embedded key set."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Destroy the lost key.",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Delete / archive the lost key's 1Password item so it cannot be used again. Record the rotation date."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "After recovery, you are back to two embedded public keys (the replacement plus a fresh reserve whose private half goes OFFLINE) - restore that two-key, one-offline posture before normal releases resume."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "operator-unavailable-and-succession",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Operator-unavailable and succession"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "strong",
            "text": "Current state: bus factor = 1."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " Frank is the sole release operator. This is the documented gap SR-2 / SEC-18 name, not a solved problem; the runbook's job is to make the fleet recoverable by someone else even while that gap stands - "
          },
          {
            "kind": "link",
            "href": "#recoverability-without-the-operator",
            "rel": "detail",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "recoverability without the operator"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " walks that worst case."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "list",
        "items": [
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "What a successor needs"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ", all listed in \"Where the keys and access live\" above: access to the 1Password vault holding key #1 (or possession of the offline key #2), the InfiniteWP admin credentials, and this runbook."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "strong",
                "text": "To actually close bus-factor-1"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " (open, Frank's decision, footfall-1f0): designate a second trusted operator and grant them standing access to the key #1 vault and InfiniteWP, so a release can be run without the primary operator. Until then, recoverability rests on the offline key #2 + a successor's physical access to it."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "id": "rotation-keep-it-cheap",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Rotation (keep it cheap)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Rotation is ceremony-free precisely because two public keys are embedded and the verifier OR-verifies, so signing can move from one key to the next across a single release with no flag day. Cheap rotation is the accepted mitigation for the laptop-exfiltration residual - rotate whenever there is any doubt rather than treating a rotation as an event."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "list",
        "ordered": true,
        "items": [
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Mint or retrieve the new keypair on a clean host; its private half is the new signing key, the prior reserve is retired."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Cut a release whose embedded public-key set includes the new public key (and, once fully migrated, drops the retired one)."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Sign with the new private key and ship the release normally - or via the InfiniteWP escape hatch if the auto-update channel is compromised."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Keep exactly two embedded public keys afterward, with the reserve key's private half stored OFFLINE (SR-7). Never bring the reserve online ahead of need; doing so only doubles the forge surface."
              }
            ]
          }
