    "details": [
      {
        "id": "what-a-compromise-buys",
        "title": "What a compromise buys",
        "source": "SEC-15, SEC-18",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Because SEC-15 pins the visitor script to hashes carried in signed releases, one valid signature also re-anchors the script channel. There is no second gate behind it - this is why custody is treated as strictly as the box's secrets."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "callout",
            "tone": "sec",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": "Accepted residual (SEC-18): the software-held key can be exfiltrated from a fully compromised, UNLOCKED laptop. This is mitigated, not solved, by signing only on a clean host and keeping rotation cheap; the "
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "link",
                    "href": "#the-hardware-bound-key",
                    "rel": "detail",
                    "spans": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "text": "hardware-bound key"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "text": " (footfall-3x8) is the deferred upgrade that would close it."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "how-or-verification-works",
        "title": "How OR-verification works",
        "source": "footfall-e98",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "The plugin's verifier ("
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "wp-plugin/src/UpdateVerifier.php"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ") checks a detached signature against the embedded set by OR: a release is valid if it verifies under "
              },
              {
                "kind": "emphasis",
                "text": "either"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " key."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "why-key-2-stays-offline",
        "title": "Why key #2 stays offline",
        "source": "SR-7",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Because the verifier OR-verifies, a key #2 that is online during normal operation merely doubles the surface an attacker can sign with."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "why-the-manifest-is-signed-too",
        "title": "Why the manifest is signed too",
        "source": "SR-5",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "The manifest is signed because plugin-update-checker renders manifest fields into wp-admin update screens through unsanitized sinks - an unsigned manifest is a box-controlled XSS channel."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "the-empty-fail-closed-seed",
        "title": "The empty fail-closed seed",
        "source": "footfall-t3z / footfall-rd5",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "No known key is in the shipped trust root; the wp-env end-to-end proof injects a throwaway key into the anchor by reflection (a test-only "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "private static"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "kind": "code",
                "text": "[]"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": " in production), so the accept path is proven without shipping anything an attacker could sign with."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "why-infinitewp-is-the-escape-hatch",
        "title": "Why InfiniteWP is the escape hatch",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "The fleet is uniform and managed via InfiniteWP: a release can be pushed to all ~30 sites manually, bypassing the signed auto-update channel the lost key can no longer feed."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "re-anchoring-mechanics",
        "title": "Re-anchoring mechanics",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "A recovery release carries a NEW embedded public-key set, so it re-anchors the fleet on keys the attacker does not hold."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "recoverability-without-the-operator",
        "title": "Recoverability without the operator",
        "source": "SR-2 / SEC-18",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "Even if the sole operator becomes permanently unavailable, the fleet is recoverable - a successor who can reach the offline key #2 and InfiniteWP can cut and push a recovery release per the key-loss recovery sequence, re-anchoring the fleet on a key they control. The offline key #2 is what makes this possible without the operator's 1Password access."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "the-hardware-bound-key",
        "title": "The hardware-bound key",
        "source": "footfall-3x8, decision 2026-06-12",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "spans": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "text": "A hardware-bound key - YubiKey 5.7 PIV ed25519 signing a small manifest, which keeps the PHP sodium verifier unchanged - would make laptop compromise alone insufficient to sign, closing the accepted software-key residual."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
